<div class="hub-intro" style="padding-top:2rem"><p>A vertical jump is the clearest single test of lower-body power. Coaches use it to track explosiveness, scouts use it to compare prospects, and players use it to answer one question: can I dunk?</p><p>Most untrained adults jump 16 to 20 inches. High school athletes who train usually reach the low to mid 20s, college athletes the high 20s to mid 30s, and NBA players average about 28 inches standing and about 34 with a running approach. Where you fall depends heavily on age, sex, sport and training history, which is why a percentile against the right group tells you more than a raw number.</p></div>
