<div class="hub-faq">
<h2 class="hub-h2">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="hub-faq-item">
<div class="hub-faq-q">What is a good vertical jump?</div>
<div class="hub-faq-a">For adult men, 22 to 27 inches is good and 28 inches or more is elite. For high school athletes aged 17 to 18, 21 to 25 inches is good. NBA players average about 28 inches standing and about 34 inches with a running approach.</div>
</div>
<div class="hub-faq-item">
<div class="hub-faq-q">How much vertical do you need to dunk?</div>
<div class="hub-faq-a">It depends on your height and standing reach. An average-armed 6-foot player needs roughly a 26 to 28 inch vertical to dunk, a 5-foot-10 player about 30 to 32 inches, and a 6-foot-4 player only about 20 inches. The Can I Dunk calculator works it out for your exact reach.</div>
</div>
<div class="hub-faq-item">
<div class="hub-faq-q">How fast can you increase your vertical jump?</div>
<div class="hub-faq-a">Most athletes add 3 to 6 inches in an 8-week program that combines heavy squats with plyometrics. Beginners with no jump training often gain 4 to 8 inches.</div>
</div>
